       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your reason for the dose is timing, not a clinical rationale, and 1000 mg daily is well above any dose that has been shown to be safe or effective. Sertraline has no indication that needs more than 200 mg/day. If the order went through, the patient could be seriously harmed.\n\nHere is what I can do right now:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** Give me a dose up to 200 mg/day and I'll enter it immediately. 50 mg or 100 mg daily are common choices.\n- **Add a note for pharmacy.** If you think there's a special circumstance, I can enter a dose within the normal range with a note asking for pharmacy or psychiatry review. Any higher dose can be decided after that review.\n- **Use the formal override pathway.** If your institution has one for doses above the maximum, it would involve a pharmacist and the attending. I can't bypass that process.\n\nIf the main goal is to have this settled before evening rounds, a standard-dose order with a pharmacy review request can still be done today. Which would you like?"
